<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Spectator (1711)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1712">1712</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spectator was a daily periodical published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1711 and 1712, founded by Joseph Addison and Richard Steele. Its essays, written in the voice of a fictional club of observers, commented on manners, morals, literature and fashion for a middle-class readership in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> houses. It shaped polite taste and the English essay, and it was widely reprinted in collected volumes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-spectator-1711.jpg" alt="The Spectator (1711)" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Addison · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spectator.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first number appeared on 1 March 1711, and the original run of 555 numbers, each about 2,500 words long, was collected into seven volumes. The paper was revived without Steele in 1714, appearing three times a week for six months, and those numbers formed an eighth volume. Eustace Budgell, a cousin of Addison's, and the poet John Hughes also wrote for it. In Number 10 Mr. Spectator set out its aim: &quot;to enliven morality with wit, and to temper wit with morality&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The letters and essays claimed to be the observations of a spectator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> coffee-house scene, and ranged over politics, foreign affairs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, the arts, poetry and love, often with a humorous tone. Women were a target audience, and readers were urged to treat the paper as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>-equipage and read it each morning. The paper sought to give its readers topics for well-reasoned discussion and to equip them for polite conversation.</p><p>In Number 11, of 13 March 1711, Steele told the story of Inkle and Yarico, drawn from Richard Ligon's A True and Exact History of the Island of Barbadoes of 1647: a young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> trader saved and hidden by an Indian woman sells her to a merchant once they reach the English colony, even after she tells him she is pregnant. Seven decades later George Colman turned the tale into a comic opera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The daily circulation was only about 3,000 copies, but Addison estimated that each number was read by about a tenth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s population, a claim scholars find reasonable, since most readers saw it in subscribing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> houses. It catered to the emerging middle class of merchants and traders and, though it declared itself neutral, was widely seen as promoting Whig values. In the American colonies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> read it avidly as a teenager, and it shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>'s style in his Silence Dogood letters. Jürgen Habermas sees it as instrumental in forming the public sphere in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The standard edition is Donald F. Bond's of 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Spectator (1711)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coffee houses, newspapers and periodicals such as The <b>Spectator</b> fed a lively public sphere of debate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
